      "problem": "If $9{x}^{2}+16=0,$ then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{16}{9}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
